# Troubleshooting

<p class="lede">
ppd's messages say what failed and what to do next. This page collects them by command, with the fix. A message
that starts with <code>refused:</code> is a safety guard (exit code 3): see the <a href="/docs/safety">safety
model</a>.
</p>

<h2 id="install">Install and dev loop</h2>

<h3 id="client-rejected">client-rejected</h3>

```text
my-plugin: client-rejected (installed)
client: the app would not evaluate this plugin's client, so none of its UI appears:
  Unknown Lucide icon: HandWaving
```

The daemon installed the plugin and its server runs, but the app would refuse the client bundle, so no panel,
screen or Command Center item shows up anywhere, and Paseo itself says nothing. `ppd install` exits 1; `ppd dev`
prints the same lines and keeps watching. Each line under `client:` is a registration rule the app enforces, for example:

- `Unknown Lucide icon: X`: the app's Lucide build has no icon named X.
- `Command Center item X has no icon`, `Workspace panel X has invalid context`, `Screen X is not a component`.
- `client.X is not part of the app's plugin client context (calling it throws)`.
- `Plugin contribution must return a cleanup function`.

Fix the line, save, and `ppd dev` re-checks on reload. `ppd check` reports the same rules as `client-eval`.

<h3 id="logs">Where the logs are</h3>

- Plugin output (`console.log` in server code, load and stop lines): `npx ppd paseo -- plugin logs <plugin id>`.
  `ppd install` prints the plugin log; `ppd dev` prints the new lines after each reload.
- The daemon's own output: `daemon.out` in the dev home that `ppd daemon status` prints.
- The browser console: `ppd web` prints console errors after every run.

<h2 id="check">ppd check</h2>

<h3 id="approximation">APPROXIMATION</h3>

```text
SKIP  paseo-compile    APPROXIMATION: no Paseo app or PASEO_SOURCE found: install Paseo, or set PASEO_APP to Paseo.app or PASEO_SOURCE to a getpaseo/paseo checkout with node_modules. Compile, evaluation and icon checks below use an esbuild replica and rules vendored from Paseo, not the host itself
```

ppd found neither the Paseo desktop app nor a Paseo checkout, so it compiled with an esbuild replica of Paseo's
build options. That is expected on Linux CI. On a Mac, install Paseo or point `PASEO_APP` (or `--paseo-app`) at it.
`--approx` forces the replica on purpose. See [Host-contract checks](/docs/check).

<h3 id="layout">could not load Paseo's plugin compiler</h3>

```text
FAIL  paseo-compile    could not load Paseo's plugin compiler from the Paseo 0.13.0 app (/Applications/Paseo.app): expected ... ppd was verified on Paseo 0.12.0 and found 0.13.0, so this release may lay things out differently. Run `ppd check --approx` for the esbuild replica, or set PASEO_SOURCE to a getpaseo/paseo checkout. Please report it at ...
```

ppd reads the compiler, the evaluator and the Lucide list from inside the installed app, which is not a public API.
A Paseo release that moves them breaks this, not your plugin. Use `ppd check --approx` or `PASEO_SOURCE`, and report
it with the message. The same pattern covers the icon list (`lucide-icons` warns and falls back), `agentTimeline`
and web UI labels. What ppd relies on is listed on [Compatibility](/docs/compatibility).

<h3 id="hermes">Hermes checks skipped</h3>

```text
SKIP  hermes-parse     hermesc not found (install react-native as a devDependency, or set HERMESC)
SKIP  hermes-vm        Hermes VM not found (react-native ships it for macOS; or set HERMES)
```

Add `react-native` as a devDependency (the templates do), or set `HERMESC` and `HERMES` to the binaries. Without
them, only `hermes-class` (no `class` syntax) guards the phone.

<h3 id="eval">The evaluation did not finish</h3>

```text
... did not finish within 30 s (an infinite loop or a blocking call at load or in contribute?)
... did not finish within 120 s (an infinite loop or a blocking call at load or in contribute?)
```

Plugin code runs in a child process with a time limit: 30 s for the client, and for the server on the
approximation; with the app or a checkout the server is evaluated inside the compile step, which has 120 s. A
blocking loop at module load or in `contribute` ends here. `plugin code called process.exit(N)` means what it says: the host would
lose the whole plugin process. A timer or server left running after the cleanup is a warning, not a failure.

<h2 id="daemon">Dev daemon</h2>

<h3 id="no-daemon">no running dev daemon</h3>

```text
no running dev daemon "default": run `ppd daemon up` first
```

`install`, `dev`, `paseo`, `web` and `android` (without `--port`) need a daemon. With `--name N` on `daemon up`, pass the same
`--name N` to the others. A record whose process is gone is cleaned up by the next `ppd daemon up`.

<h3 id="ports">Ports</h3>

```text
port 7810 is already in use: pick another --port or omit it
no free port in 7800-7899; pass --port
dev daemon "default" is already running on port 7800: run `ppd daemon down` first, or pass --name for a second daemon
```

Without `--port`, ppd takes the first free port from 7800 to 7899. `ppd daemon status --all` lists ppd's dev
daemons, and `ppd doctor` shows the running ones.

<h3 id="paseo-not-found">Paseo CLI not found</h3>

```text
Paseo CLI not found: install Paseo, put `paseo` on PATH, or pass --paseo / PASEO_BIN
```

ppd looks at `--paseo` or `PASEO_BIN`, then `paseo` on `PATH`, `~/.local/bin/paseo` and
`/Applications/Paseo.app/Contents/Resources/bin/paseo`. An explicit path that does not exist is an error, never a
fallback to another Paseo.

<h3 id="daemon-start">dev daemon exited early</h3>

The message ends with the daemon's last output and how ppd started it:
`paseo daemon run --home <home>` with `PASEO_HOME` and `PASEO_LISTEN` set and a `config.json` in the home. When
the Paseo version differs from the verified one, the message says so: a new release may have changed the flags.

<h2 id="web">ppd web</h2>

<h3 id="no-web-ui">No web UI</h3>

```text
this dev daemon has no web UI: restart it with `ppd daemon up --web-dist <dir>`
```

The web UI comes from the desktop app (`Paseo.app/Contents/Resources/app-dist`) or `PASEO_WEB_UI_DIST_DIR`.
`ppd daemon up` prints `(no web UI build found: pass --web-dist)` next to the URL when it found none.

<h3 id="no-browser">No browser</h3>

```text
no browser for Playwright: install Google Chrome, run `npx playwright-core install chromium`, or set PPD_BROWSER
```

<h3 id="workspaces">No workspace opened</h3>

```text
2 workspaces on the dev daemon, none opened: pass --workspace <id, name or dir> (ids: wks_..., wks_...)
no workspace on the dev daemon, showing the start page: `ppd daemon up --workspace <dir>` creates one
```

With one workspace, `ppd web` opens it. `paseo run --cwd DIR` creates a workspace per new directory, so fake agent
runs in several directories leave several.

<h3 id="web-labels">The web UI did not show ...</h3>

```text
the Paseo web UI did not show the workspace (a "Workspace actions" button) within 30 s. ppd finds it by the labels of the Paseo 0.12.0 web UI; a different version may have renamed it ...
```

`ppd web` finds the app shell, workspaces and the Command Center by their accessible names. On a Paseo other than
{meta.verifiedPaseo} a renamed label ends here; please report it. On {meta.verifiedPaseo}, retry with `--headed` to
watch what the page does.

<h2 id="fake-agent">Fake agent</h2>

```text
fake-agent: invalid script: ...
fake-agent refused to run this command: bash steps with run: true work only in a dev daemon started by `ppd daemon up`, and this daemon's home was not created by ppd
```

A prompt that starts with a brace or a bracket must be a valid script. Shell steps with `run: true` only run in
ppd's own daemons. See [Fake agent](/docs/fake-agent).

<h2 id="npx">npx ppd runs something else</h2>

Outside a project that has `paseo-plugin-devkit` installed, `npx ppd` resolves to an unrelated npm package named
`ppd`. Use `npx --package paseo-plugin-devkit ppd <command>`, or install the devkit as a devDependency first.
